venv/
data/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.pt
*.onnx

# --- RTL simulation artefacts (regenerated by rtl/sim/run_all.py) ----------
# Patterns are recursive (**/) rather than anchored at rtl/: the tree moved to
# hardware/rtl/ in the File-Restructuring commit, and anchored rules silently
# stopped matching. A cocotb build dir is ~4 MB and there are 20-odd of them.
**/sim/build_*/
**/sim/*.xml
**/tb/*.xml
*.vcd
*.fst
*.gtkw

# --- Synthesis run artefacts (regenerated by rtl/synth/run_genus.tcl) ------
**/synth/results/
**/synth/*.log
**/sim/final_run.log
**/synth/smoke_*.log
genus.log*
genus.cmd*
fv/

# --- Local scratch ---------------------------------------------------------
*.zip
*.jpeg


# --- Quartus build artefacts (regenerated by Start Compilation) ------------
# db/ and incremental_db/ are pure derived state and run to hundreds of MB.
# The patterns are RECURSIVE: Quartus creates one pair per project, and this
# tree has projects nested two levels deep under fpga/memcheck/.
#
# The .sof IS kept -- it is the artefact someone can actually program onto a
# board, and ~5 MB is worth the history. The .summary files are kept too:
# they hold the numbers quoted in the reports. The multi-megabyte .rpt files
# behind them are not.
**/db/
**/incremental_db/
**/output_files/
*.rpt
*.smsg
*.done
*.jdi
*.jditmp
*.pin
*.qws
*.sld
*.qdf
*.bak

# Exception: the Genus 14 nm reports are NOT build junk. They are the PPA
# evidence MILESTONE_REPORT.md quotes -- 1 GHz, +299.3 ps slack, TNS 0.0,
# 9.76 TOPS/W -- and they total ~145 KB, not the multi-megabyte Quartus
# reports the rule above targets. Without this line `*.rpt` swallows them and
# `git add` fails silently, which is how they went missing once already.
!hardware/synthesis_mac_array_1GHz/*.rpt

# --- Unzipped PyTorch checkpoint ------------------------------------------
# *.pt is already ignored above, and this directory is the same checkpoint
# with the zip container removed. Its tensor payload lives in data/, which is
# ignored, so committing it would push metadata describing weights that are
# not there -- worse than not committing it at all.
**/checkpoints/baseline_best/
